course: dictionary
lesson: 15196
title: Words that show slow movement
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Seeping is a verb. It describes a liquid that flows slowly through a tiny hole or crack. «Water was seeping in through a crack in the wall.» The water is not rushing. It creeps in, drop by drop.
explain: Seeping can also describe a liquid that is slowly leaking or soaking through something. Look at the clues around the word: tiny, slowly, drop by drop. They all point to a gentle, steady flow, not a gush.
explain: Scientists use seeping too. «Rain was seeping into the soil, moving down between the grains.» To check your meaning, swap in "flowing slowly through" and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Seep is the base word. Seeping is its -ing form, and seeped is the past tense.
fact: «Seeping» means flowing slowly through small holes or cracks.
fact: «Blood was seeping through the bandage.» In first aid, this is a sign that the wound needs more pressure.
fact: Seeping is much slower than pouring or gushing.
fact: Water moving through soil is one example of seeping in science.
exercise: «Water was seeping through a crack in the bath.» What is seeping? | Bouncing off the tiles | Frozen solid | *Flowing slowly through a tiny crack | Flowing quickly in a big rush
exercise: Sam says «seeping» means splashing. Is he right? | No, it means drying out completely | Yes, it means splashing loudly | Yes, it means sparkling | *No, it means flowing slowly through small holes
exercise: Which word in this sentence is the -ing form of seep? «The roof was leaking, and rain kept seeping in.» | rain | roof | *seeping | leaking
test: «Blood was seeping through the bandage, so Mia pressed harder.» Why did Mia press harder? | *Because the wound needed more pressure to stop the bleeding | Because the bandage was too clean | Because the blood had dried | Because she was bored
test: Which sentence uses seeping correctly? | The children ran seeping across the field | She seeped loudly down the stairs | The cake was seeping quickly in the oven | *Water was seeping out of the old pipe, drip by drip
exam: «Rain was seeping into the soil, moving slowly down between the grains.» Which word could replace seeping without changing the meaning? | Exploding | Pouring | Freezing | *Trickling
